=====================================================================
DESIGN DIRECTION — where the patterns come from
=====================================================================
The owner compared NOS against Odoo and Microsoft Dynamics 365 and picked specific patterns.
Do not copy their colours, logos or branding; the NOS palette stays.

Five principles:
  1. Navigation simple and clean like Odoo.
  2. Dashboards informative like Dynamics 365, but NOT stuffed with KPIs.
  3. Tables make big garment data easy to search, filter, sort and export.
  4. Forms: few clicks, logical grouping, clear validation.
  5. Consistency: the same interaction pattern in every module.

TAKE FROM DYNAMICS 365 (its free Fluent 2 rules):
  fixed type scale, 4px spacing grid, 3 shadows, 3 radii, layered backgrounds
  (already in tokens.css — never hard-code a size, padding, radius or shadow again);
  advanced data grid (done); FactBox / right detail pane; command bar with New / Save /
  Print / Export in the same place on every page; workspaces with count tiles; Favorites and
  Recent in navigation; toast on save, skeleton while loading, real empty states, errors beside
  the field; a visible keyboard focus ring.

TAKE FROM ODOO:
  the clean dashboard look — WHITE cards, thin border, NO coloured left edge, NO coloured chips;
  each card shows only a small label, a big number, a quiet "▲ 4.2% vs last month" and a tiny
  sparkline; widgets on an even grid, equal heights, no empty side or bottom; short list widgets
  (e.g. "Most delayed jobs") beside the charts, not only charts. This is the standard for EVERY
  dashboard. Also: a search bar with facets (Filters, Group By, Favorites); Group By with
  subtotals (buyer → job → colour); a statusbar stage bar; smart count buttons on a record
  header ("Lab dip 3", "Trims 5") that open filtered lists; a selection action bar (tick rows →
  Accept / Print / Export); breadcrumbs on drill paths only; Kanban ONLY for stage flows
  (samples Offer → SMS → PP, lab dips) offered beside the table, never replacing it;
  Undo for a few seconds after a delete.

DO NOT TAKE: Odoo's app-grid home page (the department sidebar stays); Dynamics' very dense
small type (table text never below 12px); either palette or icon style; a separate BI tool.

=====================================================================
RULES THAT APPLY TO EVERY PAGE
=====================================================================
Header and navigation
  - One compact header everywhere: page title only, no descriptive line. It scrolls away.
  - One hamburger menu, in the header. The sidebar has none of its own.
  - Dashboard pages: the page's own section buttons stay frozen, highlight left→right while
    scrolling and jump to a section on click (the Job 360 pattern).
  - A separate tab row only where the kind of work differs or there is data entry.
  - No duplicate buttons doing the same job. Where the same buttons appear in a top sub-menu
    AND inside the page, keep the INNER design the owner likes, move it to the top, delete the
    outer one.
  - No teaching text, no Bengali text, no "demo"/"prototype"/"sample data" on working screens.
    An urgent alert is one line plus one button. A "Reset demo" control may stay, ADMIN only.

Tables
  - The section's main table is the focus: full width, filling from its own top to the bottom
    of the screen, ONE vertical scroll, only the header row frozen. No half-cut rows, no empty
    space underneath, never a table inside a short scroll box.
  - Excel-style filter in every column header (tick list with counts, search, A→Z); values must
    never overlap. Filters drive cards, charts and the table together; active filters show as chips.
  - Clicking a row opens the right-side record panel. Values change only after pressing Edit.
  - As little sideways scrolling as possible, but table text never below 12px. If it does not
    fit: column chooser or column groups. Job and Style frozen left. The horizontal scrollbar
    always reachable at the bottom of the screen. One scrollbar style everywhere.
  - Small secondary tables stay in the page and scroll with it.

Look
  - Cards, charts, tables and buttons identical on every page.
  - Day mode: light surfaces, dark text. Night mode: the reverse, cards and tables dark too.
  - One date format 01.07.26. Numbers always with thousands commas and a unit (t, kg, pcs).

Charts
  - Never cards-only. Pick the chart for the data: time → trend line; compare items → column or
    bar; 2–5 parts → donut; vs target → progress bar; day × line → heatmap.
  - Only the important charts, compact, no lopsided empty space. A page may have many sections;
    each SECTION holds roughly 4–6 charts or boxes, but the page has NO overall cap.
  - Hover is calm: only the hovered mark is marked; other marks never dim or flash.
  - No label or number overlaps anything, at 1366px, 1920px and phone width.
  - Short labels ("NFL", not "In-house dyeing (NFL)"). If crowded, 2–3 small charts in one box.
  - Colours locked: blue = required / load / plan, green = delivered / done, red = balance,
    amber = in-between (grey ready, in process).
  - Always show the job count AND the quantity with its unit.
  - Keep the existing scroll-in draw-from-zero animation (components/fx).

Before building any section's dashboard, list the possible charts for it and ask the owner
which to keep. Do not decide alone.

